.TH VIEW 1
.SH NAME
view \- show meshes and pictures in a window
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B view
[
.B \-c
|
.B \-b
]
[
.B \-s
.IR w x h
]
.I scene
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I View
shows the meshes and pictures of a scene file,
.IR scene (5),
in a window, of the class view (View), which names the process of
the viewer and its machine for window managers.
A file of the scene is reloaded when it changes; a bad file is reported
and the old one kept.
The view opens fitted to all, as Space fits it, at the size the window
manager gives the window, in the view that the scene file names, or iso.
.PP
With the mouse, a left drag turns the view, a middle or right drag
moves it, and the wheel zooms about the pointer.
A click picks a part:
.BI select " name"
is printed, and the name shown in the lower left with its unit in
brackets, as front/[knob]/cap; the part is lit, and the rest of its
unit less.
A click on nothing clears the pick and prints nothing;
.B select
alone on the input clears it too.
The window's title is the path of the scene file as given.
So viewers cross-probe through FIFOs, the output of each the input of
another, as schview(1) and pcbview(1) do.
The unit of a part is the subassembly or the mesh of several parts
that holds it (the units of
.IR scene (5)),
.I level
units up: 1 at first, so that the unit of front/knob/cap is
front/knob, and of 2, front.
A shift and left drag on a part moves its unit in the plane of the
view, as
.B H
would take it.
The faces of a texture, the groups of a mesh with a
.B "# texture"
comment
.RI ( tomesh (1)),
show a grain over their color, mapped onto them along the axis nearest
their normal, the coarser the higher the grade; the shots of
.B \-b
leave it out.
.PP
Keys:
.TP 8
Space
Fit all.
.TP
F5
Read the scene and its meshes again, the view kept; Loading... stands
in a black box in the upper right corner meanwhile.
.TP
Esc
Pick nothing, and hide the help.
.TP
.B c
Center the view on the picked part, its zoom and direction kept; c
again, with no drag, zoom, view key or pick between, zooms in two times
about it.
With no part picked, nothing.
.TP
.B C
Zoom out two times about the middle of the window, centering nothing.
.TP
.B "i f b l r t u"
Iso, front, back, left, right, top, bottom.
.TP
.B h
Hide the part under the pointer.
.TP
.B o
Show only the part under the pointer; with one part alone in view,
show again the parts that showed before, wherever the pointer is
(their see-through and their moves stay as they are).
.TP
.B s
Make the part under the pointer see-through, or solid again.
.TP
.B "H O S"
The same for the unit of the part under the pointer;
.B O
shows again what showed before when that unit alone is in view.
.TP
.B "] ["
Raise the level of the unit by one, or lower it, down to 1; it stays
until changed.
.TP
.B a
Show all, all solid, all in place, with no moves.
.TP
.B "+ \-"
Step the assembly sequence: minus takes the last part in out, to the
start of its insertion path, and plus puts the next one in, so the parts
not yet in stand at their starts.
Each press, or each repeat of a held key, moves its part to the end of
its motion at once, in the next frame.
The order is that of
.IR assemble (1):
the parts of a unit, then the unit; a part with
.B with
moves with its part.
.TP
.B A
Take every part out at once, to the start of its path, as
.B "play 0"
does.
.TP
.B "^Z ^Y"
Undo, redo (of what is shown, see-through and moved; not the camera).
.TP
.B ?
Help, on or off.
.TP
.B q
Quit.
.PP
Commands are read from the standard input, one per line;
a
.I name
selects the part of that name, and a name of a unit all its parts
.IR name /...:
.PP
.RS
.nf
hide|show|only|see|solid NAME
all, reset, home, undo, redo
key h|o|s|H|O|S X Y    the key with the pointer at pixel X Y
level K                the level of the unit, 1 to 64
play K                 K motions in, 0 to all; a fraction is partway
move NAME DX DY DZ     move the part or unit NAME on, as a drag
view iso|front|back|left|right|top|bottom
fit, turn DX DY, zoom F
pick X Y               print the name there, or \-
select [NAME]          pick the part NAME, or none, printing nothing
center                 the key c
label X Y              print it with its unit in brackets, or \-
list                   NAME TRIS shown|hidden solid|see at X Y Z
shot FILE.ppm
spin N                 N frames of a turn about z
help, quit
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B Reset
is the
.B a
key,
.B home
plays the whole sequence and undoes the moves, and the position in a
list line is the center of the part's box.
.TP
.B \-c
Check the scene, print
.I "name tris"
for each part, and quit.
.TP
.B \-b
Read the commands without a window;
.B shot
then draws on the processor.
.TP
.BI \-s " w" x h
Open the window, or draw the shots of
.BR \-b ,
.I w
by
.I h
pixels, the view fitted to all at that size (640x480 by default).
.SH SEE ALSO
scene(5), tomesh(1), assemble(1)
